1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,707
That's the trunked radio pillar.

2
00:00:01,827 --> 00:00:06,866
Five ideas, and they interlock: a pool of
shared channels, a control channel

3
00:00:06,866 --> 00:00:11,904
coordinating it, talkgroups giving users a
stable identity, grants moving each call

4
00:00:11,904 --> 00:00:16,943
to its channel — and FDMA or TDMA deciding
how those channels share the spectrum.

5
00:00:17,293 --> 00:00:22,128
Everything else in trunking — multisite
networks, roaming, encryption, the

6
00:00:22,128 --> 00:00:26,964
protocol wars between P25, DMR, and TETRA
— builds on these five.

7
00:00:27,084 --> 00:00:29,499
When you're ready, those are waiting in
the Field Guide.

8
00:00:29,849 --> 00:00:34,539
Every segment's full write-up is linked
below, with diagrams and sources.

9
00:00:34,659 --> 00:00:37,751
If this helped, the whole Field Guide is
free at gophertrunk dot org — and

10
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:40,843
GopherTrunk itself, the open-source
trunking scanner this course is built

11
00:00:40,843 --> 00:00:43,935
around, is on GitHub.

12
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:44,950
Thanks for watching.
